Physical validation of the redesigned extensor route is under way on the rebuilt cam route with the \qty{20}{\mm} actuator, and the first full angle sweep is complete. An initial three-point hold near full extension on the earlier direct route measured knee torque of \qty{1.163}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{10}, \qty{7.849}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{11}, and \qty{23.437}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{10}, against hung loads of \qtylist{3.7;25.2;76}{
ewton} at gauge pressures of \qtylist{388.5;388;592}{\kPa}, with the measured wrench carried to the moving knee center by the same adjoint transform as the identification data. On the rebuilt cam route the sweep then held six isometric points from deep flexion to extension: \qty{116}{
ewton} hung at \ang{-119} and \qty{256}{\kPa}, \qty{168}{
ewton} at \ang{-61} and \qty{425}{\kPa}, \qty{181}{
ewton} at \ang{-43} and \qty{435}{\kPa}, \qty{180.2}{
ewton} at \ang{-26.2} and \qty{437}{\kPa}, \qty{181.5}{
ewton} at \ang{-30} and \qty{420}{\kPa}, and \qty{100}{
ewton} at \ang{5} and \qty{480}{\kPa}, each with its load-cell angle recorded in the tibia frame. The post-test rig measurement of the reaction-point geometry is complete, and the adjoint reduction of the six points follows in Figure~ef{fig:Ext20mm}. The corresponding flexor validation is reported with the biomimetic flexor results (Figure~ef{fig:FlxGrp}	extbf{C}).

With the reaction-point constants entered, the six holds reduce to knee-extension torques of \qty{30.24}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-119}, \qty{37.98}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-61}, \qty{38.30}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-43}, \qty{37.23}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-26.2}, \qty{38.84}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-30}, and \qty{22.86}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{5}, with the flexor-template adjoint sign convention flipped so that extension torque reads positive (Figure~ef{fig:Ext20mm}). Against the corrected prediction of the cam design evaluated at the measured pressures, the holds agree to within \qty{6.6}{
ewton\meter} at five of the six angles and exceed it by \qty{5.26}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-119}. Against the maximum-isometric vasti target at the held angles, only the deep-flexion hold at \ang{-119} meets the human target, by \qty{1.24}{
ewton\meter}, while the remaining five fall short by \qtyrange{5.0}{11.9}{
ewton\meter} at supply pressures of \qtyrange{256}{480}{\kPa}, below the \qty{620}{\kPa} design pressure at which the predicted curve exceeds the target at every held angle.

The direct extensor route exceeds the human envelope in magnitude at every angle, with zero shortfall points. Across the design range, the predicted two-BPA torque spans \qty{35.45}{
ewton\meter} to \qty{109.4}{
ewton\meter} against a human-scaled requirement of \qty{20.25}{
ewton\meter} to \qty{50.32}{
ewton\meter}, and the required overshoot is consumed as the thinnest margin exactly as designed, $+5.02$~\% at the worst-case angle of \ang{-20.2}. The route respects the wrap-release schedule of the route-elimination rule, releasing its six sequential contacts at knee angles of \ang{-89.8}, \ang{-55.7}, \ang{-47.8}, \ang{-20.2}, \ang{6.1}, and \ang{7.4}, and the optimized design places the femur-side origin at $(40.0, 137.0, 0)$~mm and the tibial insertion at $(34.4, -197.1, 0)$~mm, with a resting length of \qty{827.7}{\mm} and \qty{36.8}{\mm} of tendon.

Figure~ef{fig:FlxGrp} shows the biomimetic-knee flexor measurements with the flexor terms carried over unchanged from the pinned-knee identification. Panel 	extbf{A} presents the $\diameter$\qty{10}{\mm} actuator at $l_{0}=\qty{41.5}{\cm}$ with a \qty{21}{\mm} artificial tendon ($\diameter$\qty{1.5}{\mm} bicycle brake cable wrapped four times), pressurized to \qty{604}{\kPa}; the corrected model reached an RMSE of \qty{0.3007}{
ewton\meter}, an FVU of 0.0102, and a maximum absolute residual of \qty{0.4647}{
ewton\meter}. Panel 	extbf{B} presents the $\diameter$\qty{20}{\mm} actuator at $l_{0}=\qty{41.5}{\cm}$ with a \qty{14}{\mm} artificial tendon at \qty{620}{\kPa}, for which the corrected model reached an RMSE of \qty{1.8024}{
ewton\meter}, an FVU of 0.0499, and a maximum absolute residual of \qty{3.5678}{
ewton\meter}.

