The initial identification, fitted on the $l_{0}=\qty{48.5}{\cm}$ test, yielded a length offset $\chi_{0} = \qty{11.9}{\mm}$ and effective stiffnesses $\chi_{1} = \qty{102}{\kilo
ewton\per\meter}$ and $\chi_{2} = \qty{10.6}{\kilo
ewton\per\meter}$. The corrected model at these terms followed the measured torque across the range of motion, including the loss of torque near full extension that the rigid-body prediction did not produce, and Figure~ef{fig:FlxPin} shows this corrected-family behavior at the adopted two-stage terms. The $l_{0}=\qty{45.7}{\cm}$ actuator, configured to operate over a different joint range of motion, served as the validation test of the initial fit; the corrected prediction reproduced the measured torque there without refitting. At these initial terms, the corrected prediction for the fitting test had a root-mean-square error (RMSE) of \qty{1.616}{
ewton\meter}, a fraction of variance unexplained (FVU) of 0.0958, and a maximum absolute residual of \qty{3.2527}{
ewton\meter}; validation on the held-out actuator gave \qty{2.1214}{
ewton\meter}, 0.0878, and \qty{5.1690}{
ewton\meter}, respectively.

